      "p0001-n0006-l0000",
      "p0001-n0006-l0001",
      "p0001-n0006-l0002"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "From trained musicologists to outspoken hipsters, everyone believes they know the greatest musicians and greatest periods of music. Our goal was to use data describing artist in\ufb02uence and sound content in order to perform a quantitative analysis of the last 90 years of musical history."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000006",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0007-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0007-l0001"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0001",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0007-l0000",
      "p0001-n0007-l0001"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "First, we sought to identify the most in\ufb02uential artists by considering four distinct types of in\ufb02uence: direct, indirect, within genre, and across genre. Our key metric of in\ufb02uence was Katz"
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000007",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "title",
    "repairs": [],
    "section_id": "s0001",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0003-l0000"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "\u5fae\u4fe1\u53f7\uff1aMATHmodels"
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000008",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0007-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0007-l0003"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0001",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0007-l0002",
      "p0001-n0007-l0003"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "centrality, which we re\ufb01ned to incorporate time and directness. Focusing on 21st century music, we determined Frank Ocean had the greatest overall in\ufb02uence."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000009",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0008-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0008-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0008-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0008-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0008-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0008-l0003"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0008-l0003",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0008-l0004"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0008-l0004",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0008-l0005"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0008-l0005",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0008-l0006"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0001",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0008-l0000",
      "p0001-n0008-l0001",
      "p0001-n0008-l0002",
      "p0001-n0008-l0003",
      "p0001-n0008-l0004",
      "p0001-n0008-l0005",
      "p0001-n0008-l0006"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "We then developed a measure of musical similarity and wondered whether or not genre groupings are based on sonic characteristics. We trained a neural network to predict genres from sonic data inputs like danceability and tempo. It did not accurately predict established genres unless we limited the number of genres to the four largest. This failure inspired us to use k-means clustering on the sonic variable space to create original \u201csonic genres,\u201d offering a perspective on music which is agnostic of familiar labels. When we use this partition map on the in\ufb02uence network, we calculate a modularity of Q = 0.17."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000010",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
